From 0b0df81ac35465951aa63bfa4a9ab9339c053b66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 14:23:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/30] windows: wire the WHP backend through builder, lib and
 worker

With the WHP vstate and MMIO manager in place, this connects them to the rest
of krun-vmm and gates off the pieces that are genuinely Linux-only.

The WHP-specific paths:

* `setup_vm` takes the vCPU count, because `WHvSetupPartition` locks the
  processor count in and vCPUs can be created neither before that call nor
  after it -- neither KVM nor HVF needs this.
* The x86_64 interrupt-controller block splits by OS. WHP emulates the LAPIC
  but not the IOAPIC, so `split_irqchip` has no meaning there: the userspace
  IOAPIC is the only option and its MMIO window always goes on the bus.
* `create_vcpus_x86_64` gains a WHP variant, without the CPUID/MSR lists that
  only exist in the KVM API.
* `Payload::KernelCopy` is extended to x86_64-on-Windows. `KernelMmap` inserts
  the caller's kernel pages as an extra guest memory region via
  `MmapRegion::build_raw`, which only exists in vm-memory's Unix backend; the
  copy path is what the aarch64 and riscv64 ports have always used and it
  works here unchanged, once `arch_memory_regions` is asked for a contiguous
  layout rather than one with the kernel range carved out.

Gated off on Windows, each with the reason at the gate: virtio-vsock and TSI
(socket-based, Unix-only in krun-devices -- left gated as they were), the
linux-loader-typed error variants, and `sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE)`, which becomes
`GetSystemInfo().dwPageSize`.

Two gaps are recorded rather than papered over. Loading an *external* kernel
image returns `KernelLoaderUnavailable` naming what is missing, since every
format libkrun accepts is decoded by linux-loader. And legacy COM-port input
interrupts have no path to the guest on WHP, because the x86_64 Serial model
raises interrupts only through an eventfd bound with KVM_IRQFD; the comment on
the Windows `attach_legacy_devices` says so, and says why virtio-console (what
Boks actually uses) is unaffected.

krun-vmm on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc: 48 errors -> 0.
Host Linux (`cargo check --all-targets -p krun-vmm -p libkrun`): 0 errors.

